#!/usr/bin/env perl
#
# mail-dkim - sign a message that arrives on stdin, then hand it to sendmail(1)
# with the arguments given on the command line.
#
# It exists to be dropped where a CGI script or a cron job calls
# /usr/sbin/sendmail directly: those messages go out with no signature, so a
# forwarded copy looks exactly like a forgery to the receiving site.  Pointing
# that one call at this instead signs the mail without touching the script.
#
# The signing is in Mail::Send::DKIM; this is the command line around it, so
# that the module can be used and tested without a process and a pipe.

use strict;
use warnings;

use FindBin qw($Bin);
# Where the module is: beside this script in a checkout, and one level down
# under lib/perl5 when the distribution was installed with INSTALL_BASE - which
# is how perlbrew, local::lib and cpanm -l put it, and where @INC does not
# reach unless PERL5LIB is set for every caller, cron included.
use lib grep { -d } map { "$Bin/../$_" } qw(lib lib/perl5);
use Mail::Send::DKIM;

my $dkim = Mail::Send::DKIM->new();

my $message = do { local $/; <STDIN> } // '';
my $signed = $dkim->sign($message);
$message = $signed if(defined($signed));

my $status = $dkim->send($message, @ARGV);
$dkim->log_send($message, @ARGV) if($status == 0);

exit($status);

__END__

=head1 NAME

mail-dkim - sign a message on stdin with DKIM, then send it with sendmail

=head1 SYNOPSIS

	mail-dkim [-t] [-f address] [sendmail options...] < message

	# the way a CGI script or a crontab calls it
	DKIM_DOMAINS=example.org DKIM_KEYDIR=~/dkim \
		/usr/local/bin/mail-dkim -t -f webmaster@example.org

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Reads a complete message, headers and body, from standard input.  If the
domain in its C<From:> header is one of the domains this host signs for, a
C<DKIM-Signature> header is added and the signed message is written to
sendmail.  Otherwise the message is written to sendmail unsigned.  Either way
it is written in the local form, with LF line endings and no carriage returns,
so an MTA that writes the CRLF of SMTP itself cannot double them.

It is meant to be put in place of a direct call to sendmail(1), so that mail
sent by a script or a cron job is signed without the script being changed: a
copy forwarded by a mailing list or a colleague otherwise arrives with no
signature at all, which is indistinguishable from a forgery, and that is what
makes a DMARC policy at C<p=quarantine> or C<p=reject> expensive.

Every argument is passed to sendmail as it was given, so C<-t>, C<-f> and
C<-odq> keep working, and the exit status is sendmail's own.

The signing itself - which domains, which key, the canonicalization, and the
line endings handed to sendmail - is L<Mail::Send::DKIM>, which can be used
directly and is tested without running this programme.

=head1 ENVIRONMENT

=over 4

=item B<DKIM_DOMAINS>

A comma or space separated list of the domains to sign for, for example
C<example.org,example.net>.  A message whose C<From:> domain is not in the
list is passed through unsigned.  When this is empty or unset nothing is
signed, which is the safe state before the keys are in place.

The comparison is case insensitive, and the domain comes from the address, not
from a display name: C<< From: "Nigel" <alerts@example.org> >> is
C<example.org>.

=item B<DKIM_KEYDIR>

The directory holding the private keys.  Defaults to F<~/dkim>.  The value is
used as it stands: a relative path is taken from the working directory, and
C<~> is not expanded - the shell does that, and only when the assignment is
written unquoted.

=item B<DKIM_SELECTOR>

The selector the key is published under, which names the key file:
C<$DKIM_KEYDIR/$DKIM_SELECTOR.private>.  When it is unset the only
F<*.private> file in B<DKIM_KEYDIR> is used and its name is the selector, so a
host with one key needs no configuration beyond B<DKIM_DOMAINS>.

=item B<DKIM_KEYFILE>

The key file itself, used as it stands, for a key that is not kept under
B<DKIM_KEYDIR> in the naming convention above - a host given one key to try,
for instance.  When B<DKIM_SELECTOR> is not set the selector is the name of
this file, without the F<.private>, so setting this is an alternative to
setting both of those rather than a third thing to set with them.

=item B<SENDMAIL>

The program to hand the message to.  Defaults to F</usr/sbin/sendmail>.

=item B<HOME>

The home directory the default B<DKIM_KEYDIR> of F<~/dkim> is taken from.  When
it is unset the home directory in the password file entry is used instead.

=back

=head1 LOGGING

When sendmail accepts a message, one line is written to syslog, under the
identity C<mail-dkim> and the C<mail> facility, giving the sender, the
recipients and the subject:

	mail-dkim[1234]: sender=alerts@example.org recipient=admin@example.net subject=Disk nearly full

The sender is the address given to C<-f>, or else the one in the C<From:>
header.  The recipients are the addresses on the command line and, with C<-t>,
those in the C<To:>, C<Cc:> and C<Bcc:> headers.  Nothing is logged for a
message sendmail refused.  A syslog that cannot be written to is a warning on
standard error and nothing more: the message has gone.

=head1 BEHAVIOUR WHEN IT CANNOT SIGN

Signing never stands between a message and its recipient.  A missing or
unreadable key, several keys and no selector to choose between them, or a key
that will not load each produce a warning on standard error and the message is
delivered unsigned.

=head1 EXIT STATUS

The exit status of sendmail, so C<0> when the message was accepted.  C<1> when
sendmail cannot be run at all, and a warning on standard error.  Signing
problems do not change the exit status: the mail is still delivered.

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<Mail::Send::DKIM>, which does the signing, and F<dmarc-report.pl>, which is
what makes the reports worth signing for.

=head1 LICENCE

GNU General Public License, version 2, as in the F<LICENSE> file.

=cut
